    <bpmn:process id="W_W1330" name="W1330. Strategic Risk Scenario Planning &amp; War Gaming Exercise" isExecutable="false">
      <bpmn:documentation>Trigger: Annual strategic planning cycle; or major external event (economic downturn, new mega-competitor entry, regulatory disruption, supply chain shock); or Board request
Frequency: Annual full scenario planning exercise; quarterly tabletop exercise for highest-impact scenarios; ad-hoc for trigger events
Volume: 4–6 strategic risk scenarios developed annually; 1–2 tabletop exercises conducted per year
Owner: Head of Internal Audit (facilitator)
Participants: CEO, Executive Team, CFO, COO, VP Supply Chain, VP Merchandising, CIO, VP Legal, CHRO
Source: workflow W1330 — Strategic Risk Scenario Planning &amp; War Gaming Exercise</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:laneSet id="W1330_LaneSet3">
        <bpmn:lane id="W1330_Lane1" name="Head of IA">
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>W1330_Task5</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>W1330_Task6</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>W1330_Task7</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>W1330_Task8</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>W1330_Task10</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>W1330_Start4</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        </bpmn:lane>
        <bpmn:lane id="W1330_Lane2" name="Department Heads">
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>W1330_Task9</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>W1330_End11</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        </bpmn:lane>
      </bpmn:laneSet>
      <bpmn:startEvent id="W1330_Start4" name="Annual strategic planning cycle; or major external event (economic downturn, new…">
        <bpmn:outgoing>W1330_Flow14</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:startEvent>
      <bpmn:userTask id="W1330_Task5" name="Scenario identification">
        <bpmn:documentation>Step 1: Scenario identification: Head of IA facilitates scenario identification with Executive Team: (a) review top strategic risks from W122 risk register, (b) identify 4–6 scenarios that would have the highest impact on BuildRight&#x27;s strategy (revenue decline &gt; 20%, margin compression &gt; 5pp, or existential threat to business model), (c) prioritize scenarios that are plausible within a 3-year horizon, (d) example scenarios for a Philippine hardware retailer: (i) Mega-competitor entry: international home improvement retailer opens 20 stores in Metro Manila and Cebu within 18 months, (ii) Construction recession: government infrastructure spending cut by 40% + private construction slowdown, (iii) Import supply shock: Philippines-China trade tension disrupts 60% of import supply chain, (iv) Digital disruption: ecommerce captures 30% of hardware/DIY market within 2 years, (v) Catastrophic natural disaster: Category 5 typhoon renders 40 stores inoperable for 30+ days, (vi) Regulatory disruption: new building code requires certification for all construction materials, disqualifying 30% of current SKUs
Responsible (R): Head of IA / Executive Team
Accountable (A): CEO
Duration: 1 day</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>W1330_Flow14</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>W1330_Flow15</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:userTask>
      <bpmn:userTask id="W1330_Task6" name="Scenario development">
        <bpmn:documentation>Step 2: Scenario development: For each prioritized scenario, Head of IA develops a detailed scenario brief: (a) scenario narrative: what happens, how it unfolds over 6–12 months, cascading effects, (b) impact assessment: estimated revenue impact, margin impact, cash flow impact, headcount impact, customer impact, (c) current preparedness assessment: what existing capabilities does BuildRight have to respond? (insurance coverage, BCP plans, alternate sourcing, financial reserves), (d) key decision points: what decisions would the Executive Team need to make, and when?, (e) information requirements: what data would be needed in real-time to make those decisions?
Responsible (R): Head of IA / Department Heads
Accountable (A): CEO
Duration: 2–3 days per scenario</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>W1330_Flow15</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>W1330_Flow16</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:userTask>
      <bpmn:userTask id="W1330_Task7" name="War gaming tabletop exercise">
        <bpmn:documentation>Step 3: War gaming tabletop exercise: Head of IA facilitates a half-day tabletop exercise with Executive Team: (a) present scenario brief to participants (no prior exposure to test genuine response capability), (b) inject scenario developments in stages (e.g., &quot;Month 1: Competitor announces 5 store openings in Metro Manila&quot;; &quot;Month 3: Competitor launches aggressive pricing at 15% below BuildRight&quot;; &quot;Month 6: 3 of your Metro Manila stores see 25% revenue decline&quot;), (c) after each inject: participants discuss and decide on response actions, resource allocation, communication strategy, (d) document decisions, decision rationale, and information gaps identified, (e) after exercise: debrief with all participants — what worked, what was missing, what would we do differently?
Responsible (R): Head of IA (facilitator)
Accountable (A): CEO
Duration: Half day per scenario</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>W1330_Flow16</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>W1330_Flow17</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:userTask>
      <bpmn:userTask id="W1330_Task8" name="Preparedness gap analysis">
        <bpmn:documentation>Step 4: Preparedness gap analysis: Head of IA compiles findings from the exercise: (a) Information gaps: data that was needed but not available in real-time (e.g., competitor pricing intelligence, store-level daily profitability, customer churn rate), (b) Decision-making gaps: decisions that could not be made because authority was unclear or process was too slow, (c) Capability gaps: responses that were desired but not currently possible (e.g., rapid ecommerce scaling, rapid store format conversion, vendor diversification), (d) Communication gaps: stakeholders that needed to be informed but no communication channel existed, (e) Financial preparedness: insurance coverage gaps, insufficient cash reserves, inability to access emergency credit facilities
Responsible (R): Head of IA
Accountable (A): CEO
Duration: 2–3 days</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>W1330_Flow17</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>W1330_Flow18</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:userTask>
      <bpmn:userTask id="W1330_Task9" name="Action plan &amp; implementation">
        <bpmn:documentation>Step 5: Action plan &amp; implementation: For each identified gap: (a) assign remediation owner and deadline, (b) prioritize remediation: Critical (must be addressed within 6 months), Important (within 12 months), Beneficial (within 24 months), (c) track remediation progress quarterly as part of W122 ERM cycle, (d) examples of remediation actions: (i) establish competitor intelligence monitoring process, (ii) pre-negotiate emergency credit facility with bank, (iii) develop rapid ecommerce scaling playbook, (iv) establish strategic reserve of critical import inventory (90-day supply), (v) create pre-approved crisis communication templates
Responsible (R): Department Heads
Accountable (A): CEO
Duration: Ongoing (tracked quarterly)</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>W1330_Flow18</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>W1330_Flow19</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:userTask>
      <bpmn:userTask id="W1330_Task10" name="Board reporting">
        <bpmn:documentation>Step 6: Board reporting: Head of IA presents scenario planning findings and preparedness status to Board Risk Committee: (a) scenarios exercised and key findings, (b) preparedness gaps and remediation progress, (c) recommendations for Board approval (e.g., capital allocation for strategic reserves, insurance coverage changes, authorization of emergency credit facility), (d) integrate into annual ERM report per W122
Responsible (R): Head of IA
Accountable (A): Board Risk Committee
Duration: Absorbed into W122 quarterly cycle</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>W1330_Flow19</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>W1330_Flow20</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:userTask>
      <bpmn:endEvent id="W1330_End11" name="Complete">
        <bpmn:incoming>W1330_Flow20</bpmn:incoming>
      </bpmn:endEvent>
      <bpmn:textAnnotation id="W1330_Annotation12">
        <bpmn:text>Controls: - CTL-305 (ensure controlled execution — Enterprise Risk Management (PA-21.2).) | - operational: linking to annual strategic planning cycle when Exec Team is already in planning mode; operational: using historically grounded scenarios (e.g., Yolanda-level typhoon, actual competitor entries in other ASEAN markets); operational: Board Risk Committee oversight and quarterly tracking via W122
Owner: Head of Internal Audit (facilitator)</bpmn:text>
      </bpmn:textAnnotation>
      <bpmn:association id="W1330_Assoc13" sourceRef="W1330_Annotation12" targetRef="W1330_Start4" associationDirection="None" />
      <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="W1330_Flow14" sourceRef="W1330_Start4" targetRef="W1330_Task5" />
      <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="W1330_Flow15" sourceRef="W1330_Task5" targetRef="W1330_Task6" />
      <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="W1330_Flow16" sourceRef="W1330_Task6" targetRef="W1330_Task7" />
      <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="W1330_Flow17" sourceRef="W1330_Task7" targetRef="W1330_Task8" />
      <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="W1330_Flow18" sourceRef="W1330_Task8" targetRef="W1330_Task9" />
      <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="W1330_Flow19" sourceRef="W1330_Task9" targetRef="W1330_Task10" />
      <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="W1330_Flow20" sourceRef="W1330_Task10" targetRef="W1330_End11" />
    </bpmn:process>
    <bpmndi:BPMNDiagram id="W1330_diag">
